{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"COORDINATION HAZARD between this ticket and sibling b5fe — recorded as a COMMENT rather than an AC edit because this ticket's plan review is currently IN FLIGHT and editing its material mid-review is what caused 9227's first sign to fail with stale-pin-drift. Fold it into the ACs only after the review lands, if the reviewer has not already raised it.\n\nBOTH b300 AND b5fe TIGHTEN THE SAME ASSERTION. tests/unit/test_plan_review_workflow_ops_seam.py (shipped by d8ef) currently reads:\n\n    for path in (_WORKFLOW_OPS, _LLM_CONFIG):\n        loc = _loc(path)\n        assert loc < _HARD_CAP\n\nOne loop, one shared bound, two files. b300's AC tightens llm/config.py to < 700 and b5fe's AC tightens plan_review/workflow_ops.py to < 700. Because the bound is SHARED, tightening it for either file also tightens it for the other — and the other is still ~793 until its own extraction lands. So the first of the two to land would go RED on its sibling's file.\n\nREQUIRED SEQUENCING: whichever of b300/b5fe lands FIRST must split that loop into PER-FILE bounds (e.g. an explicit (path, bound) table) and set only its own file to 700, leaving the sibling at the staged 800. The second ticket then changes exactly one number. This is cheap if anticipated and a confusing red CI if not — the failure would name the OTHER ticket's file, so whoever hits it would look in the wrong place.\n\nFound by reading b5fe's criteria as a reviewer before submitting it, not by a gate. b5fe's copy of this note is already in its AC text."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785574329292563001,"uuid":"c1c1f75b-0fae-4129-b558-8b493af9871c"}